          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "diagnose a values clash, predict whether \"this isn't fair\" is really about the",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#moral-psychology",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "outcome or the process, and notice when a team is talking itself into conduct it",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#moral-psychology",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "would normally reject.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#moral-psychology",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "It is descriptive, not prescriptive. It tells you how moral cognition works;",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#moral-psychology",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "it never tells you what is actually right (see Theme 7). And it is the moral",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#moral-psychology",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "lens specifically - for the neighboring lenses, see When to use something else.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#moral-psychology",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "When this skill fires",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Reach for it when the question is about rightness, values, or fairness, not",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#when-this-skill-fires",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "\"Two stakeholders agree on every fact and still won't converge - why?\"",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#when-this-skill-fires",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "\"The customer keeps saying it's unfair, but the number is objectively market-rate.\"",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#when-this-skill-fires",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "\"I've made the rational case three times and the champion won't budge.\"",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#when-this-skill-fires",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "\"They're treating a victimless change as a moral betrayal. Where's the victim?\"",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#when-this-skill-fires",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "\"This decision is mathematically right but feels viscerally wrong to everyone.\"",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#when-this-skill-fires",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "\"Is our team rationalizing something we shouldn't be doing?\"",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#when-this-skill-fires",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The map: four questions decide which lens",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Which value is in play? → Moral Foundations / Morality-as-Cooperation (T1)",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#the-map-four-questions-decide-which-lens",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Why won't reasoning move them? → Social Intuitionist Model (T2); dual-process (T3)",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#the-map-four-questions-decide-which-lens",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Who is the harmed party in their head? → Theory of Dyadic Morality (T4)",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#the-map-four-questions-decide-which-lens",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Is this fair - and are we rationalizing? → Organizational Justice (T6); moral disengagement (T5)",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#the-map-four-questions-decide-which-lens",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Theme 7 (is–ought) bounds what you may ethically do with any of the above.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#the-map-four-questions-decide-which-lens",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "1. Moral Foundations Theory — *which* value governs the call",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Moral judgments run on a few intuitive foundations: **Care/harm,",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Fairness/cheating, Loyalty/betrayal, Authority/subversion, Sanctity(purity)/",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "degradation, plus a proposed Liberty/oppression**. They cluster into",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "individualizing (care + fairness - protect the person) vs binding (loyalty",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "+ authority + sanctity - protect the group). Best-replicated result: a **political",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "asymmetry** - progressives lean on care + fairness; conservatives weight all",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "roughly equally; libertarians weight liberty highest. Measured by the MFQ → MFQ-2.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Contested - say so. Factor analysis frequently recovers only two clean",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "factors, not five, and the rival Morality-as-Cooperation (Curry 2019) derives",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "seven cooperative domains from game theory and claims better grounding. Treat",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "MFT as a working vocabulary, not settled fact.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> Operator application. A values-based stakeholder conflict is usually a fight",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> over which foundation applies (a \"fairness\" framing vs a \"we honor our",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> commitments / loyalty\" framing), not over the facts. **Name the foundation in",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> play** instead of adding more evidence - evidence aimed at the wrong foundation",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#1-moral-foundations-theory-which-value-governs-the-call",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "2. Social Intuitionist Model — intuition first, reasoning is the press secretary",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Haidt 2001: moral judgment is fast intuition first; conscious reasoning is",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "mostly post-hoc justification - the \"press secretary, not the judge.\" But the",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "model is social: reasoned persuasion and others' stances can reshape a",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "person's intuition (links 3/5/6), so reasoning has limited but real force —",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "mostly through people and over time, not by winning the argument in the moment.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Evidence: moral dumbfounding (\"it's wrong, I just can't say why\" - the Julie &",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Mark vignette). Caveat: the original dumbfounding study was an unpublished,",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "n≈30 demonstration; later work supports the phenomenon but debates its rate.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> Operator application. When purely rational arguments bounce off, it's",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> because the intuition came first and reason is defending it. **Reframe the",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> intuition (engage a different gut read) or use social/relational channels**",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> (a trusted peer moves the intuition more than your logic does) rather than",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#2-social-intuitionist-model-intuition-first-reasoning-is-the-press-secretary",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "3. Dual-Process Moral Judgment — the emotional vs cognitive split",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Greene: an automatic/emotional route pushes toward deontological judgments",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "(\"it's just wrong to push him\"), a controlled/cognitive route toward",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "utilitarian ones (\"5 > 1\"). Trolley/switch (impersonal) → most permit;",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "footbridge (personal, hands-on) → most refuse, despite identical math.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Cognitive load selectively slows utilitarian judgments; VMPFC-lesion patients",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "are more utilitarian.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Contested - attach the caveats. Kahane 2015: sacrificial \"utilitarian\" answers",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "track reduced harm-aversion (Instrumental Harm), not genuine **Impartial",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Beneficence** (Oxford Utilitarianism Scale) - so \"more utilitarian on trolley\" is",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "not \"more moral.\" The CNI model (Gawronski 2017) shows the dichotomy conflates",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Consequences-sensitivity, Norms-sensitivity, and a general Inaction bias.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Trolley vignettes also draw heavy ecological-validity criticism.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> Operator application. When a decision is mathematically sound but feels",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> viscerally wrong (a cold-formula layoff, a technically-fair clawback), expect",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> deontological recoil that no spreadsheet beats. Reduce the",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> personalness/directness of the harm, or wrap it in fair process (Theme 6) —",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> don't just re-present the arithmetic.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#3-dual-process-moral-judgment-the-emotional-vs-cognitive-split",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "4. Theory of Dyadic Morality — find the victim in their head",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Schein & Gray 2018: moral cognition is organized by a template - an **intentional",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Agent harming a vulnerable Patient (A→P). Two phenomena: moral typecasting**",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "(agents and patients seen as opposites - villains feel less pain, victims can do",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "no wrong) and dyadic completion (for a \"victimless\" wrong, people **invent a",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "victim** - society, the relationship, future users, their own integrity).",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Contested vs MFT on whether all morality reduces to harm - unresolved.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> Operator application. When a stakeholder frames a victimless change as a",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> moral breach, they've already cast someone as harmed (their team, their",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> users, the relationship) and you as the intentional agent. Surface **who",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> they perceive as harmed** before debating intent - \"no one was hurt\" just gets",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> the template re-completed.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#4-theory-of-dyadic-morality-find-the-victim-in-their-head",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "5. Moral Licensing & Moral Disengagement — how people excuse bad acts",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Moral licensing (Monin & Miller 2001): a past good act licenses a later",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "questionable one. It is small and fragile - state this plainly: meta-analytic",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "d≈0.31 with strong publication-bias signals → bias-corrected to **d≈0.18 or",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "near null, and a large registered replication found ≈zero. Do not build a plan",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "around inducing it.**",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Moral disengagement (Bandura 1999) is the actionable one - eight mechanisms",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "for switching off self-sanction, used as a detection checklist: ① moral",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "justification ② euphemistic labeling ③ advantageous comparison ④ displacement of",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "responsibility ⑤ diffusion of responsibility ⑥ distortion/disregard of",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "consequences ⑦ dehumanization ⑧ attribution of blame.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> Operator application. When a team slides toward conduct it would normally",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> reject (misleading a customer, burying a known defect), listen for these eight",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> verbal tells. Naming the mechanism out loud (\"that's euphemistic labeling —",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> say what we're actually doing\") re-engages the self-sanction the language was",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#5-moral-licensing-moral-disengagement-how-people-excuse-bad-acts",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "6. Organizational Justice — fairness is process + treatment, not just outcome",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "The highest-impact theme. Colquitt 2001's four dimensions:",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "distributive (the outcome - equity theory; equity/equality/need norms),",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "procedural (the process - Leventhal's six rules: **consistency,",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "bias-suppression, accuracy, correctability, representativeness, ethicality** +",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "voice), interpersonal (dignity and respect), and informational (honest,",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "adequate explanations).",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "The fair-process effect is the single most useful finding: a **fair process",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "raises acceptance of even an unfavorable outcome.** People accept a price increase,",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "a denied feature, or a lost escalation they dislike if the process was",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "consistent, gave them voice, treated them with dignity, and was **honestly",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "explained** - and resent a good outcome delivered arbitrarily.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> Operator application. \"This isn't fair\" is rarely only about the dollar",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> amount (distributive). It's usually process (no warning, no voice,",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> inconsistent with last time) and treatment (curt, no real explanation). You",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> often can't change the outcome - but you can almost always improve **process and",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> explanation**: advance notice, voice, consistent rule, candid reasons, an appeal",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> path. That is the lever that turns \"unfair\" into \"I don't love it, but I get it\"",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> - in pricing changes, escalations, negotiations, and missed-SLA conversations.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#6-organizational-justice-fairness-is-process-treatment-not-just-outcome",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "7. Is–Ought / Naturalistic Fallacy — the limit on ethical nudging",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Everything above is descriptive. Hume: you cannot derive an ought from an",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#7-isought-naturalistic-fallacy-the-limit-on-ethical-nudging",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "is. Moore (Principia Ethica, open-question argument): \"good\" is not",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#7-isought-naturalistic-fallacy-the-limit-on-ethical-nudging",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "definable by any natural property. Describing how moral cognition works **never",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#7-isought-naturalistic-fallacy-the-limit-on-ethical-nudging",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "prescribes** what is right - disgust driving purity judgments doesn't make them",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#7-isought-naturalistic-fallacy-the-limit-on-ethical-nudging",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "correct, and fairness perception being hackable via process doesn't make it",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#7-isought-naturalistic-fallacy-the-limit-on-ethical-nudging",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> Operator application. This bounds the ethics of nudging: use moral",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#7-isought-naturalistic-fallacy-the-limit-on-ethical-nudging",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> psychology to understand and communicate honestly, not to manufacture",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#7-isought-naturalistic-fallacy-the-limit-on-ethical-nudging",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> consent or steer people past their own judgment. When someone argues \"it's",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#7-isought-naturalistic-fallacy-the-limit-on-ethical-nudging",
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "text": "> natural, therefore it's fine,\" flag the is→ought gap.",
          "source": "https://llms-explorer.com/sources/mdb-context-hub/moral-psychology/#7-isought-naturalistic-fallacy-the-limit-on-ethical-nudging",
          "note": null
        }
      ]
    },
